  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NEOTROP MIGRANTS VI</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The American Bird Conservancy and its in-country partners will plant 112,000 native trees and coffee bushes; acquire 577 acres; and contribute to the ongoing protection of about 30,000 acres as significant wintering habitat for migratory birds in Ecuador and Peru. These efforts will focus on the Canada warbler and olive-sided flycatcher, but will benefit 20 additional neotropical migratory bird species, as well as many resident bird species. Activities will also engage coffee growers and cattle ranchers, through technical workshops and outreach, to improve bird habitat on their lands, and encourage people living on public lands within the Alto Mayo Protection Forest to sign conservation agreements, empowering local people to use agricultural practices that benefit migratory birds.</narrative>
  </description>
